WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 178: Harry S. Truman: The Whistle-Stop Miracle and the Korean War (1948–1953)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.240
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our Harry S.

4
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:15.680
[Rebecca] Truman saga with one of the greatest underdog triumphs in political folklore, followed by one of the most perilous crises of the Cold War.

5
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:24.560
[Rebecca] In an era when nuclear confrontation threatened to engulf the globe, Truman stood unyielding against Soviet expansion and military insubordination.

6
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:31.200
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the whistle stop miracle, the Berlin Airlift, and the standoff with MacArthur.

7
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:43.120
[George] In the immediate aftermath of World War II, the fragile wartime alliance between Washington and Moscow shattered into the bitter rivalry of the Cold War.

8
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:53.760
[George] Joseph Stalin broke his Yalta pledges, establishing brutal Soviet-dominated communist police states across Poland, Romania, Hungary, and Bulgaria.

9
00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:06.640
[George] In March 1947, when communist insurgencies threatened to topple Greece and Turkey and Great Britain announced it could no longer provide financial aid, Truman stepped before a joint session of Congress.

10
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:19.600
[George] In a historic address, he articulated the Truman Doctrine, declaring that it must be the policy of the United States to support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or outside pressures.

11
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.480
[George] Paired with Secretary of State George C.

12
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:33.920
[George] Marshall's visionary Marshall Plan, which poured $13 billion into rebuilding the shattered economies of Western Europe, Truman contained the spread of communism without firing a shot.

13
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:46.480
[George] In June 1948, when Stalin imposed a total land blockade on West Berlin to starve the enclave into submission, military advisers urged Truman to force a tank corridor through East Germany.

14
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:53.200
[George] Truman refused to risk World War III, ordering instead the magnificent Berlin airlift.

15
00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:06.280
[George] For eleven months, American and British pilots flew 278,000 flights, landing every 90 seconds at Tempelhof Airport to deliver two million tons of food, coal and medicine.

16
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.320
[George] Humiliating Stalin and saving West Berlin.

17
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:21.240
[George] The Truman Doctrine and Marshall Plan established the indispensable strategic pillar of containment that prevented Soviet communism from overrunning Western Europe during its hour of greatest vulnerability.

18
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:32.040
[George] This courageous logistical triumph demonstrated to the entire world that the Western democracies would defend human freedom against communist intimidation with relentless ingenuity.

19
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:41.960
[George] In 1948, Harry Truman took a moral stand that risked the utter annihilation of his political career, championing civil rights for African Americans.

20
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:58.440
[George] Horrified by reports of black World War II veterans in uniform being lynched and blinded by racist mobs in the South, Truman issued a landmark report titled, To Secure These Rights, demanding an end to lynching, poll taxes, and Jim Crow segregation.

21
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:21.000
[George] In July 1948, bypassing a recalcitrant Congress, Truman signed historic executive orders 9980 and 9981, officially desegregating the Federal Civil Service and the entire United States armed forces, the most significant federal action for racial equality since Abraham Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation.

22
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.880
[George] The political backlash was catastrophic.

23
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:34.760
[George] Southern segregationist Democrats, led by South Carolina Governor Strom Thurmond, bolted from the Democratic Convention in Philadelphia to form the Dixiecrat Party.

24
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:42.440
[George] Simultaneously, left-wing Democrats deserted to form the Progressive Party behind former Vice President Henry Wallace.

25
00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:55.040
[George] Stripped of both his Southern base and progressive wing, Truman entered the 1948 presidential campaign with his party shattered into three pieces and his public approval rating hovering at 36%.

26
00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:05.920
[George] By desegregating the United States military, Harry Truman took a monumental moral stand that laid the indispensable groundwork for the modern African American civil rights movement.

27
00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:14.880
[George] In taking this principled stand, Truman proved that the moral conscience of the presidency must never be compromised for regional political expediency.

28
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.200
[George] Every major newspaper, commentator, and pollster in America regarded Republican nominee Thomas E.

29
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.440
[George] Dewey as the guaranteed victor.

30
00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:34.480
[George] Dewey, the polished, mustache-wearing governor of New York, ran an aloof, dignified campaign designed to avoid mistakes, mouthing platitudes about unity and efficiency.

31
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:44.800
[George] Truman, possessing no money, virtually no newspaper endorsements, and abandoned by party bosses, decided to take his case directly to the American people.

32
00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:58.800
[George] Boarding the private presidential railroad car Ferdinand Magellan, Truman embarked on an epic 31,000-mile whistle stop tour across the continent, delivering 352 speeches over ten grueling weeks.

33
00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:07.280
[George] Speaking without prepared scripts from the rear platform of the train, Truman spoke in the raw, pungent vernacular of ordinary citizens.

34
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:20.560
[George] He assaulted the Republican-controlled do-nothing, good for nothing 80th Congress, warning farmers, factory workers, and union members that Republicans would repeal the new deal and plunge the nation back into depression.

35
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:28.880
[George] When a working-class supporter shouted from the crowd, give 'em hell, Harry, Truman famously barked back, I don't give him hell.

36
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.440
[George] I just tell the truth on them, and they think it's hell.

37
00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:44.360
[George] The legendary whistle stop tour proved that authentic human contact, passionate conviction, and plain speaking could triumph over the combined weight of money, media elites, and conventional wisdom.

38
00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:58.840
[George] On election night, November 2, 1948, the outcome seemed so predetermined that the Chicago Tribune sent its morning edition to press with the colossal banner headline, DWIDFEATSTRUMAN.

39
00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:00.520
[George] Radio commentator H.

40
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:00.840
[George] V.

41
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:11.160
[George] Coltonborn announced on the air that despite early Truman leads in rural precincts, Dewey was certain to sweep the Electoral College as the major urban vote came in.

42
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:21.240
[George] Truman, having eaten a ham sandwich and drank a glass of milk at a mineral bath hotel in Excelsior Springs, Missouri, went calmly to bed at 9 o'clock.

43
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.520
[George] Waking at 4 in the morning, his Secret Service agents informed him that he had carried Ohio, Illinois, and California.

44
00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.040
[George] Truman had won a miraculous, staggering victory.

45
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.520
[George] 303 electoral votes to Dewey's 189.

46
00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.600
[George] Two days later, standing on the rear platform of his train at St.

47
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:50.920
[George] Louis Union Station, a beaming Truman held up a copy of the Chicago Tribune emblazoned with its erroneous headline, grinning from ear to ear for photographers.

48
00:06:50.920 --> 00:07:02.200
[George] The photograph became the most iconic image of political vindication in American history, proving forever that in a democracy, the people, not the experts, decide their destiny.

49
00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:04.520
[George] That triumphant morning in Synth.

50
00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:13.720
[George] Louis stands as an eternal reminder that the ballot box belongs to the sovereign will of ordinary citizens, not to the predictions of professional pundits.

51
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:35.600
[George] Truman's second term was abruptly engulfed by war on June 25, 1950, when 135,000 Soviet-equipped North Korean troops launched a surprise invasion across the 38th parallel, rapidly overrunning South Korea's capital of Seoul and pinning American and South Korean forces against the sea in the Pusan perimeter.

52
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:43.440
[George] Remembering the failure of the League of Nations to resist fascist aggression in the 1930s, Truman acted with breathtaking speed.

53
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:54.880
[George] Boycotting Soviet delegates were absent from the United Nations Security Council, allowing Truman to win authorization for a multilateral military intervention led by the United States.

54
00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:10.480
[George] Appointing General Douglas MacArthur as Supreme Commander of UN forces, Truman committed American ground troops without seeking a formal congressional declaration of war, framing the conflict as a police action to prevent a global nuclear third world war.

55
00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:20.080
[George] In September 1950, MacArthur executed a masterpiece of military daring, the amphibious landing at Incheon behind North Korean lines.

56
00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:30.720
[George] In two weeks, UN forces liberated Seoul, shattered the North Korean army, and pursued the fleeing remnants north toward the Yalu River on the border of Communist China.

57
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:42.320
[George] Truman's courageous intervention in Korea established the vital principle of collective security, demonstrating that unprovoked communist aggression would be met with resolute international resistance.

58
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:54.720
[George] At a summit on Wake Island in October 1950, MacArthur personally assured President Truman that the Chinese communists would never intervene, boasting that American boys would be home by Christmas.

59
00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.600
[George] It was a catastrophic intelligence blunder.

60
00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:12.760
[George] In late November, 300,000 Chinese troops under General Peng Di Huey struck through the freezing mountains of North Korea, trapping the 1st Marine Division at the Chazin Reservoir and forcing the longest retreat in American military history.

61
00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:20.360
[George] As the war stalemated along the 38th parallel, MacArthur grew furious with Truman's policy of limited war.

62
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:30.920
[George] MacArthur publicly demanded permission to bomb Chinese bases in Manchuria, blockade the Chinese coastline, and unleash nationalist Chinese forces from Taiwan.

63
00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.800
[George] Even advocating the use of atomic weapons.

64
00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:47.480
[George] When Truman ordered all military commanders to clear public statements through the White House, MacArthur openly defied his commander-in-chief, releasing a letter to House Republican leader Joseph Martin sabotaging peace negotiations.

65
00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:53.880
[George] On April 11, 1951, Truman took the most politically perilous decision of his life.

66
00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:57.800
[George] He fired General Douglas MacArthur for insubordination.

67
00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.400
[George] A wave of furious public outrage swept America.

68
00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:08.120
[George] Truman was burned in effigy, bombarded with 100,000 hate letters, and threatened with impeachment.

69
00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.000
[George] Yet Truman stood firm on the bedrock principle of the American Constitution.

70
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:17.400
[George] Civilian control of the military must remain absolute and inviolate.

71
00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:30.680
[George] In removing General MacArthur from command, Truman courageously risked his own political survival to defend the foundational American principle that the military must always remain subordinate to elected civilian authority.

72
00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:31.640
[George] When Harry S.

73
00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:46.000
[George] Truman left office in January 1953, the Korean War was locked in an unpopular, grinding stalemate, and his public approval rating had fallen to 22%, the lowest ever recorded by Gallup in polling history.

74
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:58.400
[George] Returning to independence, Missouri, Truman had no corporate board offers, no secret wealth, and received no federal pension until Congress passed the former President's Act in 1958.

75
00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:06.880
[George] He spent his final two decades living modestly in his Victorian home, taking daily brisk morning walks and writing his memoirs.

76
00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:14.160
[George] Yet as the decades passed and the passions of the Cold War cooled, history delivered a stunning reassessment of Harry Truman.

77
00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:35.040
[George] The institutions he created, the Marshall Plan, the Truman Doctrine, the Berlin Airlift, the United Nations, NATO, the CIA, the National Security Council, and the desegregation of the military formed the indestructible foundation that ultimately won the Cold War and preserved world peace without a nuclear cataclysm.

78
00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:45.680
[George] The Haberdasher from Missouri who never sought power had proven that ordinary character, moral courage, and fidelity to democratic duty are the highest qualities of leadership.

79
00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:57.120
[George] Harry Truman departed Washington as one of the most unpopular presidents of modern times, yet history has vindicated his courageous decisions as the bedrock of global peace and freedom.

80
00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:58.320
[Rebecca] Harry S.

81
00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:06.080
[Rebecca] Truman left the presidency, having preserved civilian democracy and constructed the global framework of the Western Alliance.

82
00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:17.600
[Rebecca] To succeed him, the American people turned to the most celebrated military hero of the 20th century, the supreme allied commander who had liberated Europe from Adolf Hitler.

83
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.400
[Rebecca] Next week, we begin our exploration of Dwight D.

84
00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:30.360
[Rebecca] Eisenhower, the Kansas farm boy, West Point graduate, architect of Operation Torch and D-Day, and the beloved leader known to millions simply as Ike.

85
00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:34.520
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

